{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgnRtt3JssmYsYjd78CFq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I'm connecting the early-layer input changes (from L0 and L1, which read the embeddings) with the late output changes (from L16 and L17, which write the logits), since both should lie in a shared subspace of embedding space that corresponds to the answer tokens. To capture that, I'm assembling a subspace T from the top components of the late writers and early readers, and looking for what they have in common.\n\nI'm working out a principal subspace shared by the two sets of directions, using either an SVD of the Gram matrices or a canonical correlation between orthonormal bases, so that the top canonical directions mark the shared answer-token subspace. Then I'm planning a copy test for each head: projecting the OV circuit's copy term onto that subspace and taking the trace, with care over how embedding-space directions map into the residual stream.\n\nI'm checking that the answer-token subspace T also captures anti-diagonal behavior, where a model that guesses \"yes\" should output \"no.\" In a binary set, that would mean the copy form along the difference direction e_yes − e_no comes out negative.\n\nI'm checking how the copy-versus-flip signal along the yes–no difference direction carries over into the dimension count of T, and I'm planning to run CCA to read off the correlations.\n\n","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4539,"cache_read_input_tokens":63603,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4539},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c9fb2689-71d1-4789-9324-4ca53bd1b486","uuid":"71